Public health organizations need an outbreak management system to support investigation, monitoring, analysis, and reporting during disease outbreaks, bioterrorism incidents, and other health events. Investigators should be able to collect information about cases, contacts, exposures, specimens, and interventions, connect related entities and events, and use the resulting information to identify and contain a health threat while protecting sensitive data.

Current Slots:
[
  {
    "name": "project_goals",
    "category": "goals",
    "value": "Enable investigators to quickly see what is happening in an outbreak, connect cases and exposures, and act early enough to interrupt transmission; provide decision-makers with a reliable operational picture to prioritize resources, track response progress, and support defensible public health decisions; reduce missed links, speed investigation and reporting turnaround, improve team coordination, and protect sensitive information to maintain trust and consistent use."
  },
  {
    "name": "stakeholder_roles",
    "category": "stakeholders",
    "value": "Primary users are public health investigators, disease surveillance staff, and outbreak response coordinators who enter, link, and analyze case and contact information; decision-makers and supervisors use the system for situational awareness, trend review, and reporting; lab and field partners may contribute information or receive assignments and need secure role-based sharing of relevant information."
  },
  {
    "name": "functional_needs",
    "category": "functionality",
    "value": "Fast data entry; track relationships across cases, exposures, specimens, and interventions; clear workflow for follow-up and status updates; summarized views, timelines, and current-data reporting for decision-makers; secure sharing of information relevant to each user role."
  },
  {
    "name": "business_rules",
    "category": "business_logic",
    "value": "Each contact, exposure, specimen, and intervention must be tied to the correct case or event, and relationship history must be preserved when records are updated. Urgent or high-risk cases should be surfaced immediately for the assigned team and moved into active follow-up status. Incomplete records should be flagged and kept visible until required information is provided or explicitly deferred."
  },
  {
    "name": "data_entities",
    "category": "data",
    "value": "Cases, contacts, exposures, specimens, interventions, relationship links between people and events, status updates, timelines, summarized reporting data, urgent/high-risk flags, incomplete record indicators, and relationship history."
  },
  {
    "name": "constraints",
    "category": "constraints",
    "value": "Must comply with privacy and public health data-sharing rules, with tightly controlled access by role and jurisdiction; must remain usable during surges, handle spotty field connectivity, and support incomplete or rapidly changing data; exact regulatory standards and load thresholds still need verification."
  },
  {
    "name": "quality_concerns",
    "category": "quality",
    "value": "System must protect sensitive information appropriately, be reliable enough to support trusted use, and enable fast turnaround for investigation and reporting."
  },
  {
    "name": "boundary_conditions",
    "category": "exceptions",
    "value": "Outbreak operations may begin with incomplete facts and changing data; the system must support field use with spotty connectivity and high-volume surges during emergency response."
  },
  {
    "name": "user_access_control_model",
    "category": "security",
    "value": "Role-based secure access with information shared only according to user role; lab and field partners should only receive or contribute the information relevant to their responsibilities."
  },
  {
    "name": "case_contact_exposure_workflows",
    "category": "functionality",
    "value": "Support workflow for entering, linking, and analyzing case and contact information; follow-up and status updates; relationship tracking across cases, exposures, specimens, and interventions."
  },
  {
    "name": "reporting_and_dashboard_needs",
    "category": "reporting",
    "value": "Summarized views, timelines, situational awareness, trend review, and reporting for decision-makers and supervisors, with confidence that data is current."
  },
  {
    "name": "integration_with_external_systems",
    "category": "integration",
    "value": "Integrations may be needed with surveillance systems, laboratory reporting systems, and possibly immunization or vital records systems; data exchange should avoid duplicate entry and keep partner systems aligned with the current record."
  },
  {
